## Why This Is Asked
Real-world KCNA deployments must tolerate broker failures while preserving delivery guarantees and avoiding duplicates during recovery. This question probes practical handling of offsets, in-flight state, and replay safety.

## Key Concepts
- Durable per-tenant commit logs
- In-flight state snapshots for unacked events
- Idempotent event processing with eventId deduplication
- Per-tenant replay guards and safe replay windows
- Chaos testing to validate recovery paths

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: idempotent processing with dedup on eventId
class KCNAConsumer {
  constructor(store){ this.dedup = store; }
  async handle(event){
    if (await this.dedup.has(event.id)) return; // dedup
    await this.apply(event);
    await this.dedup.set(event.id, true);
    await this.commitOffset(event.offset);
  }
  async apply(e){ /* apply business logic per tenant */ }
  async commitOffset(o){ /* persist committed offset per tenant */ }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Fair sharing of resources across tenants is crucial in multi-tenant KCNA streams. This task validates per-tenant isolation, offset handling, and basic backpressure at beginner level.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant rate limiting, token bucket
- Cross-topic consumption and offset commits
- At-least-once semantics and fault tolerance
- Canary testing for bursts

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: per-tenant limiter in consume loop
let buckets = new Map<string, {tokens:number, lastRefill:number}>();
function allow(tenantId){
  const now=Date.now();
  const b = buckets.get(tenantId) ?? {tokens:0, lastRefill:now};
  const elapsed=(now-b.lastRefill)/1000;
  b.tokens = Math.min(200, b.tokens + 100*elapsed);
  b.lastRefill = now;
  buckets.set(tenantId,b);
  if(b.tokens>=1){ b.tokens-=1; return true;} return false;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adjust for highly bursty tenants or skewed workloads?
- How would you persist quotas across restarts and redeployments?"

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of access control in KCNA, multi-tenant isolation, and auditable, low-friction token rotation.

## Key Concepts

- Tenant-scoped ACLs and per-topic allowlists
- Token-based authentication with runtime rotation
- Non-blocking authorization checks and audit logs
- Backward-compatible ACL evolution

## Code Example

```javascript
// ACL data model and quick middleware example
const acl = {
  "tenantA": { topics: ["t1","t2"], actions: ["publish","subscribe"], version: 1 }
}
function authorize(tenant, action, topic, tokenVersion) {
  const entry = acl[tenant];
  if (!entry) return false;
  return entry.version === tokenVersion && entry.topics.includes(topic) && entry.actions.includes(action);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you store ACLs to support auditability and rotation without downtime?
- How would you test for token revocation affecting in-flight requests?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a streaming gate with per-user policy, token verification, per-user caches, and idempotence considerations under multi-tenant load.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming token validation per user
- TTL-based per-user caches
- Exact vs at-least-once semantics in gates
- Canary rollout and rollback safety

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: gate events with per-user tokens
function processEvent(e, tokenStore) {
  const t = tokenStore.get(e.userId);
  if (!t) return {forward:false, reason:'no-token'};
  if (Date.now() >= t.expiry) return {forward:false, reason:'expired'};
  if (!verifySignature(e, t.signature)) return {forward:false, reason:'bad-signature'};
  return {forward:true};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would token revocation during peak load affect latency and correctness?
- How would you test clock skew and cross-partition consistency for token expiry?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to handle schema evolution in a streaming pipeline, ensuring backward/forward compatibility and correct event-time handling under retries.

## Key Concepts
- KCNA versioned deserialization
- Event-time preservation
- Per-id deduplication with TTL
- End-to-end at-least-once semantics
- Canary tests with mixed versions

## Code Example
```javascript
function normalizeAuditEvent(raw){
  const obj = JSON.parse(raw);
  const v = obj.version ?? (obj.actor===undefined ? 1 : 2);
  if(v === 1){
    return { id: obj.id, ts: obj.ts, action: obj.action, actor: null };
  }
  return { id: obj.id, ts: obj.ts, action: obj.action, actor: obj.actor };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test and roll this out with a mixed-version producer pool?
- How would you scale the dedupe cache across multiple consumer instances?

